export const landmarks = {
'bom-jesus': {
 name: 'Bom Jesus do Monte Sanctuary', short: 'A Baroque hillside staircase, basilica and water-powered funicular from 1882',
 long: 'Pilgrims climb a zigzag Baroque staircase lined with chapels and fountains. Work on the staircase began in 1722; the basilica designed by Carlos Amarante was completed in 1811. The neighbouring funicular, opened in 1882, uses the weight of water in its tanks. The sanctuary joined the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2019.',
 history: 'A chapel of the Holy Cross stood on Mount Espinho as early as 1373. It was rebuilt in 1494 and 1522. In 1629, local people founded the brotherhood of Bom Jesus do Monte, building a church, accommodation for pilgrims and small Passion chapels.\n\nArchbishop Rodrigo de Moura Teles began the great transformation in 1720. From 1722, the entrance, Stations of the Cross and Stairway of the Five Senses took shape. Water flows from the eyes, ears, nose or mouth of the fountain figures. Chapels and fountains attributed to André Soares followed in the 1760s. Around 1781, Archbishop Gaspar de Bragança added the Stairway of the Three Virtues: Faith, Hope and Charity.\n\nThe new church was begun on 1 June 1784 to a design by Carlos Amarante and completed in 1811. It is among Portugal’s early neoclassical churches. The staircase climbs 116 metres. In 1882, a funicular designed by Niklaus Riggenbach and built under Raoul Mesnier du Ponsard opened alongside it: the descending, water-filled car pulls the other uphill.\n\nThe church became a minor basilica in 2015. On 7 July 2019 the sanctuary was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Its wooded park welcomes pilgrims and visitors; the staircase was conceived as a spiritual journey as well as an ascent.',
 facts: ['The staircase climbs 116 metres in 583 steps.', 'Its fountains represent the five senses and three virtues: Faith, Hope and Charity.', 'The 1882 funicular was the first on the Iberian Peninsula.', 'Each funicular car carries 38 passengers and has a 5,850-litre water tank.', 'The staircase inspired sanctuaries in Lamego and Congonhas, Brazil.'],
 tip: 'TUB bus 2 runs from Avenida da Liberdade to Bom Jesus. Take the funicular up and walk down the staircase to see the fountains. Sameiro is about 2 km away.',
 panorama: { caption: 'Chapels and flights of the Baroque staircase, the square in front of the basilica and the church interior' },
 gallery: ['The nave of Bom Jesus basilica, looking towards the altar', 'The central aisle leading to the basilica’s high altar', 'A car on the steep track of the 1882 water-powered funicular', 'The zigzag pilgrimage staircase with the basilica above'],
},
'sameiro': {
 name: 'Our Lady of Sameiro Sanctuary', short: 'A major Marian sanctuary on a hilltop above Braga',
 long: 'Founded in 1863 by Father Martinho da Silva, Sameiro is one of Portugal’s principal places of Marian devotion after Fátima. The basilica stands above Bom Jesus, with views across the Braga valley. Pope John Paul II celebrated Mass here in 1982.',
 history: 'Father Martinho António Pereira da Silva proposed a monument to the Virgin after Pope Pius IX proclaimed the dogma of the Immaculate Conception in 1854. The foundation stone was laid on 14 June 1863; the first monument was blessed in 1869.\n\nThe statue of Our Lady of Sameiro, carved in Rome by Eugenio Maccagnani, was brought into a new chapel in 1880. In 1904 it was crowned with a gold crown donated by the women of Portugal. Work on the present church began on 31 August 1890 and continued for decades. Construction of the dome began in 1936; the high altar was consecrated in 1941.\n\nLook for the polished granite altar, silver tabernacle and Querubim Lapa’s tile panels in the crypt. John Paul II celebrated a Mass for families here on 15 May 1982, and the sanctuary received the papal Golden Rose in 2004. Large pilgrimages take place on the first Sunday of June and the third Sunday of August. The hill rises more than 350 metres above Braga.',
 facts: ['The statue was carved in Rome and arrived in Braga in 1878.', 'Its 2.5 kg gold crown was donated in 1904.', 'John Paul II celebrated a Mass for families here on 15 May 1982.', 'The sanctuary received the papal Golden Rose in 2004.', 'Sameiro is less than 2 km from Bom Jesus and 4 km from central Braga.'],
 tip: 'Combine Sameiro with Bom Jesus: the wooded road between them is under 2 km, around half an hour on foot or a few minutes by taxi. A clear late afternoon offers the best valley views.',
 gallery: ['The basilica nave and altar of Our Lady', 'The crypt beneath the basilica', 'The Nativity tile panel in the crypt', 'A Latin commemorative plaque inside the basilica'],
},
'se-braga': {
 name: 'Braga Cathedral', year: '11th century', short: 'Portugal’s oldest cathedral, founded before the kingdom itself',
 long: 'Construction began in the 11th century. Romanesque walls acquired Gothic, Manueline and Baroque additions over the centuries. The Kings’ Chapel holds the tombs of Count Henry and Teresa, parents of Portugal’s first king, Afonso Henriques. Two gilded 18th-century organs still accompany worship.',
 history: 'The cathedral stands over Roman remains and an early Christian basilica. Following the restoration of the bishopric around 1070, Bishop Pedro began the new building and consecrated it on 28 August 1089, before the foundation of the Portuguese kingdom.\n\nIts Romanesque design drew on Burgundy and Cluny. Construction continued into the 13th century, followed by Gothic and Manueline additions. Archbishop Diogo de Sousa commissioned João de Castilho to build the main chapel. Later alterations brought a Baroque façade and a dome above the crossing.\n\nInside, visit the Kings’ Chapel, with the tombs of Henry of Burgundy and Teresa of León. Simão Fontanes built the two gilded organs in 1737 and 1739. Look for the Romanesque south portal, the Porta do Sol, and the Flemish tomb of Prince Afonso, son of João I. The cathedral remains a working church, with worship in the distinctive Braga rite; the former chapter house contains the Treasury Museum.',
 facts: ['Bishop Pedro consecrated the cathedral on 28 August 1089.', 'The Archbishop of Braga holds the ancient title Primate of the Spains.', 'Mass is celebrated here in the distinctive Braga rite.', 'Simão Fontanes built the organs in 1737 and 1739.', 'The cathedral has been a national monument since 1910.'],
 tip: 'Visit in the morning. The cathedral’s published hours are 08:00–13:00 and 14:30–18:30. Allow an hour for the separate chapels and Treasury visit. Arco da Porta Nova is a three-minute walk away.',
 gallery: ['The cathedral nave between its gilded organs', 'Two 18th-century Baroque organs above the choir', 'The Gothic vault and stained glass of the main chapel', 'Carved woodwork in the upper choir'],
 panorama: { caption: 'A school visit through the cathedral’s rooms and chapels; pupils appear in the frame, with a logo at the bottom' },
},
'arco-porta-nova': {
 name: 'Arco da Porta Nova', short: 'The western gateway to the old town, which never had doors',
 long: 'Commissioned by Archbishop Gaspar de Bragança in 1772, this arch replaced a gate from 1512. It marks the western entrance to the historic centre. It never had doors, inspiring the Portuguese joke to someone who leaves a door open: “Are you from Braga?”',
 history: 'In 1512, Archbishop Diogo de Sousa opened a new gateway through Braga’s medieval walls, leading to the main shopping street, Rua do Souto. Its name, Porta Nova, means “New Gate”.\n\nThe present arch was commissioned in 1772 as the city grew beyond its defences. The design is usually attributed to André Soares, although some historians name Carlos Amarante. The masonry was carried out by Francisco Tomás Correia. It became a national monument in 1910.\n\nCompare its two faces. The late Baroque side towards the centre bears Our Lady of Nazareth. The more restrained outer face has Archbishop Gaspar’s arms and an allegorical figure of Braga. A surviving medieval wall tower is accessible through the nearby Image Museum. The arch’s lack of doors lives on in the local joke, “Are you from Braga?”',
 facts: ['The original gateway dates from 1512; the current arch from 1772.', 'Its lack of doors inspired the saying “Are you from Braga?”', 'The inner face is Baroque; the outer face is neoclassical.', 'The architect is disputed: usually André Soares, sometimes Carlos Amarante.', 'It has been a national monument since 1910.'],
 tip: 'Walk through in both directions to compare the façades. The cathedral is three minutes away and Biscainhos Museum two minutes away, making this a useful starting point.',
 gallery: ['The statue at the top of the arch', 'The arch viewed from the old town', 'Stone pinnacles seen from above'],
 panorama: { caption: 'A walk through the centre: the arch appears at about 1:25, followed by the old town’s pedestrian shopping street' },
},
'santa-barbara': {
 name: 'Santa Bárbara Garden and Old Episcopal Palace', year: '14th–18th centuries', short: 'Flower beds beneath the crenellated walls of the medieval archbishops’ palace',
 long: 'The garden lies beside the palace’s old wing, framed by Gothic arches and battlements. Tulips and box hedges fill it with colour in spring. A fountain with a statue of Saint Barbara stands at its centre. Built between the 14th and 18th centuries, the palace now houses the public library and University of Minho rectorate.',
 history: 'The garden borders the palace’s oldest, eastern wing. Braga’s archbishops ruled the city after Henry and Teresa granted it to them in 1112. Archbishop Gonçalo Pereira began the palace in 1336; Fernando da Guerra expanded it in 1422–1436, with the tower completed by 1439.\n\nLater wings were added in the 16th and 18th centuries. A fire in 1866 destroyed the Baroque wing and library. The archbishops left in 1911, and Braga Public Library opened here on 1 December 1934. Today the palace also houses the University of Minho rectorate.\n\nThe garden is much younger: José Cardoso da Silva designed it in 1955. Geometric beds, box hedges and clipped trees surround a 17th-century octagonal fountain brought from the Convent of Remédios. Medieval arches line the flower beds. The garden received municipal heritage recognition in 2018.',
 facts: ['The garden dates from 1955; the palace was begun in 1336.', 'Its 17th-century fountain came from the Convent of Remédios.', 'The 1866 fire destroyed the palace’s Baroque wing and library.', 'Henry and Teresa granted Braga to the archbishops in 1112.', 'Braga Public Library opened in the palace on 1 December 1934.'],
 tip: 'Spring and early summer bring the brightest flower beds. Come in the morning for softer light and fewer people. Largo do Paço, Rua do Souto and Praça da República are nearby.',
 gallery: ['Gothic arches of the old palace in the garden', 'Flower beds and clipped box hedges in front of the palace', 'The crenellated palace wing above the flowers', 'A carved stone detail among the flower beds'],
},
'praca-republica': {
 name: 'Republic Square and Arcada', year: '18th century', short: 'Braga’s central square, with arcades, a fountain and historic cafés',
 long: 'This is Braga’s lively meeting place. The Arcada and Lapa Church form its western edge. The arches date from 1715, while the current building was completed in 1885. Vianna and Astória cafés sit beneath the arches, with A Brasileira around the corner. The castle keep rises behind the square.',
 history: 'The square developed outside the eastern medieval wall and became a food market in the late 16th century. It was called Largo da Lapa from 1761, Largo Hintze Ribeiro from 1904 and Praça da República after the republic was proclaimed in 1910. Locals simply call it the Arcada.\n\nAn early arcade commissioned by Diogo de Sousa was replaced in 1715 by Rodrigo de Moura Teles’s 19 arches. Lapa Church was inserted in the centre during the 18th century, its bell tower resting on a medieval wall tower. The current arcade building, designed by municipal engineer Joaquim Pereira da Cruz, was completed in 1885.\n\nThe square became a garden in the 19th century and once carried steam and electric trams. Cars were removed and an illuminated fountain installed in 1994–1995. Vianna and Astória remain longstanding meeting places; writers Eça de Queirós and Camilo Castelo Branco visited Vianna. Nearby A Brasileira opened in 1907.',
 facts: ['The 1715 arcade has 19 arches: eight on either side and three in the centre.', 'Lapa Church’s bell tower rests on a medieval wall tower.', 'In 1921, Café Vianna issued vouchers when small change was scarce.', 'The square took its present name in 1910.', 'A Brasileira opened on 17 March 1907; it once required a tie.'],
 tip: 'Have a morning coffee under the arches and watch the city wake up. The keep is two minutes away, Theatro Circo five minutes down Avenida da Liberdade, and the cathedral ten minutes along Rua do Souto.',
 gallery: ['The vault and chandelier of Lapa Church inside the Arcada', 'Café Vianna beneath the arches in the evening', 'The Arcada building and square’s fountain'],
 panorama: { caption: 'A 30-second view of the fountain and surrounding square on a sunny day' },
},
 'theatro-circo': {
 name: 'Theatro Circo', short: 'An ornate 1915 theatre on Avenida da Liberdade',
 long: 'Opened in 1915, the theatre quickly became a source of civic pride. Gold decoration, red velvet and a painted ceiling make its auditorium one of Portugal’s most admired. It reopened after extensive restoration in 2006 and hosts theatre, concerts, dance and cinema.',
 history: 'A group of citizens led by Artur José Soares proposed a larger theatre in 1906, because the small São Geraldo theatre could not accommodate major companies. They chose land on Avenida da Liberdade formerly belonging to the Convent of Remédios.\n\nJoão de Moura Coutinho designed the building, constructed in 1911–1914. It opened on 21 April 1915 with Leoncavallo’s operetta The Queen of Roses, starring Palmira Bastos. The Italian-style auditorium originally held 1,500 people and hosted opera, circus and cinema; sound films arrived in 1930.\n\nThe municipality bought the building in 1988. A seven-year restoration led by Sérgio Borges began in 1999, ending with a reopening concert on 27 October 2006. Look for Domingos Costa’s 12-by-8-metre curtain and Benvindo Ceia’s 1915 ceiling painting of dancing nymphs. The main auditorium now seats almost 900, alongside a smaller hall and the Salão Nobre.',
 facts: ['The theatre opened on 21 April 1915 with The Queen of Roses.', 'It originally held 1,500 people; the main auditorium now seats almost 900.', 'The first sound films were shown here in 1930.', 'A seven-year restoration ended with reopening on 27 October 2006.', 'It joined the European Route of Historic Theatres in 2015.'],
 tip: 'Guided tours last 45 minutes and are offered in Portuguese or English. Tickets are sold at the box office 30 minutes before the tour; check dates at theatrocirco.com. The square is a five-minute walk away.',
 gallery: ['The main auditorium: tiers, chandelier and painted ceiling', 'The painted auditorium dome and chandelier', 'The grand hall with pink columns and gilding', 'A velvet sofa in the theatre foyer'],
},
'palacio-raio': {
 name: 'Raio Palace', short: 'André Soares’s Baroque palace with a blue-tiled façade',
 long: 'André Soares designed the palace for merchant João Duarte de Faria. Blue tiles frame its elaborate granite decoration. It is named after later owner Miguel José Raio. Today it houses a museum of the history of Braga’s Misericórdia charitable brotherhood.',
 history: 'Wealthy merchant João Duarte de Faria commissioned the palace in 1752–1754. André Soares designed its late Baroque architecture, with the light, asymmetrical ornament characteristic of his work.\n\nMiguel José Raio, Viscount of São Lázaro, bought it in 1853 after making his fortune in Brazil. He added the blue tiles and gave the house its lasting name. The Santa Casa da Misericórdia bought it in the 1880s for services of neighbouring São Marcos Hospital, which remained here until 2011.\n\nNotice how the tiles set off the grey granite windows, cornices and central portal. Painted ceilings survive above the staircase, and a late 19th-century stained-glass door separates it from the vestibule. Following restoration, the palace reopened in 2015 as the Misericórdia Memory Centre: ten rooms display religious art, ceramics, jewellery, an old pharmacy and archival records.',
 facts: ['André Soares designed the palace, built in 1752–1754.', 'Miguel José Raio bought the house in 1853 and gave it his name.', 'The blue façade tiles were added in the 19th century.', 'São Marcos Hospital services occupied the palace until 2011.', 'Since 2015, its ten-room museum has presented the history of the Misericórdia.'],
 tip: 'The palace is five minutes south of Praça da República. The museum is closed on Sundays and Mondays. Fonte do Ídolo is just around the corner.',
 gallery: ['The palace’s grand staircase with blue tiles', 'An 18th-century tile scene inside the palace', 'A display in the Misericórdia history museum', 'The vestibule with granite arches'],
},
 'torre-menagem': {
 name: 'Braga Castle Keep', year: '14th century', short: 'The surviving tower of the medieval castle, in the city centre',
 long: 'Built in the early 14th century under King Dinis, the keep was the main tower of Braga’s castle. The castle walls were demolished in the early 20th century as the city expanded. The granite tower and its battlements still overlook Praça da República.',
 history: 'Around 1300, King Dinis ordered a castle at Braga’s northern approach. Its rectangular plan included corner towers, two gate towers and a central keep; the walls were nearly three metres thick.\n\nAfter Castilian troops sacked Braga in 1369, King Fernando strengthened the defences. Battlements and machicolations were added to the keep. Later windows made it suitable for habitation, and the castle housed a prison in the 15th century. The square tower rises about 30 metres over four floors. Some 533 masons’ marks have been identified on its blocks.\n\nThe castle lost its military role in the 18th century and was demolished on 15 November 1905, an event celebrated with bands and a procession. The keep and neighbouring church tower survived. The remains became a national monument in 1910; a city history centre opened in the keep in 2017.',
 facts: ['The keep is about 30 metres high and has four floors.', 'Around 533 masons’ marks have been found on its stones.', 'Castilian troops sacked the poorly defended city for six days in 1369.', 'The castle’s demolition on 15 November 1905 was celebrated with a procession.', 'The castle remains have been a national monument since 1910.'],
 tip: 'Check visitbraga.travel before visiting: the source listing reports the history centre as temporarily closed. The tower is easy to see from Terreiro do Castelo, two minutes from the Arcada.',
 gallery: ['The granite keep and its battlements', 'Masons’ marks on the tower’s blocks', 'A view across the old town rooftops from the tower'],
},
'santa-cruz': {
 name: 'Santa Cruz Church', short: 'A church with an elaborately carved Baroque granite façade',
 long: 'The Brotherhood of the Holy Cross began building the church in 1625. Its façade is among Braga’s most ornate, with granite carved as delicately as lace. Gilded woodwork fills the interior. Theatro Circo is only a short walk away.',
 history: 'The lay brotherhood of Santa Cruz financed the church. Archbishop Afonso Furtado de Mendonça attended the laying of the foundation stone in 1625. Masonry was completed in 1653 and the two bell towers in 1694.\n\nStructural cracks soon appeared. Work begun by Manuel Fernandes da Silva in 1731 was abandoned; in 1734 the body of the church was dismantled, leaving only the façade. The new building was finished in 1739.\n\nOn the façade, look for the instruments of the Passion and roosters recalling Peter’s denial, which gave rise to the nickname “church of the roosters”. Saint Helena holds a cross at the top. João VI granted the brotherhood royal status in 1822. Inside are a stone coffered vault, gilded pulpits and an organ commissioned from Galician maker Miguel Mosquera in 1742. The brotherhood continues its charitable work.',
 facts: ['The foundation stone was laid in 1625; the façade bears the date 1642.', 'The roosters on the frieze recall the apostle Peter’s denial.', 'João VI granted the brotherhood royal status in 1822.', 'Galician maker Miguel Mosquera built the 1742 organ.'],
 tip: 'Stand across Largo Carlos Amarante for a full view of the façade, including its roosters and statues. Theatro Circo and Raio Palace are three to four minutes away.',
 gallery: ['The nave and gilded altars of Santa Cruz Church', 'Gilded carving beneath the vault', 'The church’s Baroque organ', 'A gilded carved angel on a pilaster'],
},
'estadio-braga': {
 name: 'Braga Municipal Stadium', short: 'Eduardo Souto de Moura’s stadium carved into a granite quarry',
 long: 'The stadium is cut into an old granite quarry, with just two stands and a bare rock face behind one goal. Steel cables support the roofs, inspired by Inca rope bridges. It opened in 2003 for Euro 2004 and is home to Sporting Braga.',
 history: 'Eduardo Souto de Moura designed the stadium in 2000 for the 2004 European Championship, with structural engineering by Rui Furtado of AFAconsult. Built in 2002–2003, it opened on 30 December 2003 with Braga playing Celta.\n\nIts site is an old quarry on Mount Castro’s northern slope in Dume. Stands run along only the sides of the pitch: one end faces rock, the other the city and Cávado valley. This setting explains its nickname, A Pedreira, “The Quarry”. Steel cables inspired by Inca bridges join the two roofs, with a gap admitting sunlight to the grass. A 5,000-square-metre space beneath the pitch connects the stands.\n\nThe project won the Secil Prize; Souto de Moura received the Pritzker Prize in 2011. Construction far exceeded the initial €29.9 million estimate. Bulgaria–Denmark and Netherlands–Latvia were played here during Euro 2004. The municipally owned stadium seats 30,286 and hosts Sporting Braga.',
 facts: ['The stadium seats 30,286 and has only two stands.', 'Steel roof cables were inspired by Inca rope bridges.', 'The opening match was Braga–Celta on 30 December 2003.', 'It hosted Bulgaria–Denmark and Netherlands–Latvia at Euro 2004.', 'Eduardo Souto de Moura received the Pritzker Prize in 2011.'],
 tip: 'The stadium is around 1.5 km north of the centre, roughly 20 minutes on foot. Check scbraga.pt for weekday tours. The quarry face is also visible from the surrounding park.',
 gallery: ['The stand and pitch, with the quarry face behind the goal', 'Stairs and concrete supports beneath the stand'],
 panorama: { caption: 'A school visit from the edge of the pitch, looking towards the stand and roof, then the changing room; a logo appears at the bottom' },
},
 'termas-romanas': {
 name: 'Roman Baths of Alto da Cividade', year: '1st–2nd centuries AD', short: 'The public baths of Roman Bracara Augusta',
 long: 'Braga began as the Roman city of Bracara Augusta. Archaeologists discovered its public baths on Cividade hill in 1977. The remains include cold, warm and hot rooms and an underfloor heating system. A Roman theatre was also excavated nearby.',
 history: 'The Romans founded Bracara Augusta around 16 BC. The growing city gained a forum, temples, a theatre and public baths. The baths on Cividade hill were built in the early 2nd century alongside the theatre.\n\nDiscovered in 1977, the site was excavated for more than twenty years by the University of Minho. In 1999, the search for the edge of the exercise yard, or palaestra, revealed the neighbouring theatre. The baths were repeatedly enlarged and reorganised before being abandoned in the early 5th century.\n\nFollow the sequence from changing room to cold, warm and hot rooms. Low brick columns supported the floor, allowing hot air from a furnace to circulate beneath it: the hypocaust system. The site opened to visitors in November 2004 and is a national monument.',
 facts: ['The baths were built in the early 2nd century alongside the theatre.', 'Discovered in 1977, they were excavated by the University of Minho.', 'The neighbouring Roman theatre was discovered in 1999.', 'The baths operated for about three centuries, until the early 5th century.', 'The site has welcomed visitors since November 2004.'],
 tip: 'The baths are on a quiet hill ten minutes southwest of the cathedral. They are closed on Sundays and public holidays. A combined ticket includes Fonte do Ídolo; the nearby D. Diogo de Sousa Museum adds context.',
 gallery: ['Excavated bath rooms beneath their protective roof', 'Hypocaust columns that allowed hot air beneath the floor', 'Wall and pool foundations of the Roman baths'],
},
'fonte-idolo': {
 name: 'Fonte do Ídolo', year: '1st century AD', short: 'A Roman spring sanctuary carved directly into granite',
 long: 'This 1st-century sanctuary is carved into the granite around a spring. A figure in a toga and Latin inscriptions remain visible. Celicus Fronto commissioned them in honour of the local deity Tongoenabiagus. A modern museum pavilion protects the spring beside Raio Palace.',
 history: 'The spring was a place of water and fertility worship before the Romans arrived. When Bracara Augusta was founded around 16 BC, it became part of the new city.\n\nAround the turn of the era, Celicus Fronto, a newcomer from Arcobriga, commissioned carved figures and Latin inscriptions dedicating the spring to Nabia and Tongoenabiagus. His descendants later added a stone basin. Look for the figure in a toga, the bust in a classical niche and the words TONGOE NABIAGOI.\n\nThe historian Jerónimo Contador de Argote published an engraving of the fountain. It was classified as a national monument in 1910. A protective pavilion built in 2001–2004 now houses a small museum with a film explaining the carvings.',
 facts: ['The spring was revered before Roman Braga was founded.', 'Celicus Fronto of Arcobriga commissioned the carvings and inscriptions.', 'The inscriptions honour Nabia and Tongoenabiagus.', 'It became a national monument on 16 June 1910.', 'The protective pavilion was built in 2001–2004.'],
 tip: 'Enter from Rua do Raio, opposite Raio Palace. Allow 20–30 minutes. A combined ticket includes the Roman baths, about ten minutes away on foot.',
 gallery: ['The Latin inscription of Celicus Fronto of Arcobriga', 'A figure in a toga and an inscription on the granite', 'Carvings in the rock above the spring', 'The museum room beside the sanctuary'],
},
 'tibaes': {
 name: 'São Martinho de Tibães Monastery', year: '17th–18th centuries', short: 'The former mother house of the Benedictines in Portugal and Brazil',
 long: 'Founded in the 11th century, the monastery’s present buildings date from the 17th and 18th centuries. It directed Benedictine monasteries throughout Portugal and Brazil. André Soares contributed to its gilded church interior. The state acquired it in 1986; it now includes a museum, gardens and woodland.',
 history: 'The first monastery was founded between 1070 and 1077 in a green valley about 5 km west of Braga. In 1110, Henry and Teresa granted lands and privileges allowing it to collect taxes and administer justice.\n\nTibães became the mother house of the Benedictine congregation in 1567. The complex was rebuilt in the 17th and 18th centuries. Manuel Álvares and João Turriano’s church was finished in 1661. André Soares designed its high altar, pulpits and side altarpieces in 1757–1760; the monk José de Santo António Vilaça carved and gilded them.\n\nFollowing the dissolution of the monasteries in 1834, the property was sold. A fire in 1894 destroyed the refectory cloister, kitchens and dormitories. The state bought the ruined complex in 1986 and began restoration. Visit the Rococo chancel, cloisters and garden staircase leading to Saint Benedict’s chapel. Tibães became a national monument on 30 October 2024.',
 facts: ['It became the Benedictine mother house for Portugal and Brazil in 1567.', 'André Soares designed the altar and pulpits in 1757–1760.', 'A fire destroyed the refectory cloister and dormitories in 1894.', 'The state acquired the ruined monastery in 1986.', 'It became a national monument on 30 October 2024.'],
 tip: 'The monastery’s published hours are Tuesday–Sunday, 10:00–19:00 in April–September and 10:00–18:00 in October–March; closed Mondays. Listed admission is €4, or €1.50 for the garden. A taxi is convenient; allow at least an hour for the grounds.',
 gallery: ['The monastery church nave with gilded carving', 'Baroque altars of Tibães in daylight', 'A window with stone seats in the monastic rooms', 'Cloister arcades and church towers', 'A granite staircase beside the monastery courtyard'],
},
'biscainhos': {
 name: 'Biscainhos Museum', year: '17th century', short: 'An aristocratic palace and Baroque garden revealing 18th-century life',
 long: 'Built in the 17th century and remodelled in the 18th, the palace is now a museum furnished as if its owners had just stepped out. Tiles, painted ceilings, furniture and porcelain fill the rooms. A Baroque garden with fountains and hedges lies behind the house.',
 history: 'The palace was built in the early 17th century for Constantino Ribeiro do Lago. Its name comes from the street, where craftsmen from Biscay had settled in the 16th century to work on Braga’s buildings.\n\nIn 1712, cathedral dean Francisco Pereira da Silva commissioned Manuel Fernandes da Silva to enlarge the house. The Baroque garden was laid out in the same decade, and Manuel Furtado de Mendonça painted the main hall ceiling in 1724. Later owners included the Counts of Bertiandos and Viscounts of Paço de Nespereira.\n\nThe last private owner sold the palace to the local authorities. The museum opened on 11 February 1978, presenting aristocratic life from the 17th to 19th centuries through furniture, porcelain, silver and tableware. Look for the tiled staircase and painted ceilings before stepping into the garden, an outdoor continuation of the rooms.',
 facts: ['Built in the early 17th century, the palace was enlarged after 1712.', 'Its street was named for craftsmen from Biscay.', 'The main hall ceiling was painted in 1724.', 'The museum opened on 11 February 1978.', 'The Baroque garden was laid out in the 1710s.'],
 tip: 'The palace is two minutes from Arco da Porta Nova. The museum is closed on Mondays. Leave time for the garden, especially in spring and summer.',
 gallery: ['A room with 17th–18th-century furniture, carpets and earthenware', 'A drawing room with paintings and antique tables', 'A sequence of palace rooms', 'The gateway and Baroque garden behind the palace'],
 panorama: { caption: 'A walk through Braga: the first roughly 80 seconds show the Biscainhos garden, lawns, box hedges, fountain and pinnacled walls' },
},
'avenida-central': {
 name: 'Avenida Central and Avenida da Liberdade', year: '19th–20th centuries', short: 'Braga’s main boulevards, with fountains, trees and city life',
 long: 'Avenida Central extends east from Praça da República as a long garden boulevard with fountains and benches. Avenida da Liberdade runs south from the square. Both are places for evening walks and celebrations, including Braga’s São João festivities in June.',
 history: 'Avenida Central began as an open space laid out under Archbishop Diogo de Sousa in the early 16th century. It was called Campo de Santana after a nearby chapel of Saint Anne.\n\nThe square changed names several times, including a period commemorating soldiers of the First World War. Its western end meets Praça da República and the Arcada. The source account describes it as larger than Porto’s Avenida dos Aliados and Lisbon’s Terreiro do Paço.\n\nTo the south, Avenida da Liberdade follows the former Rua das Águas. It received its “Freedom Avenue” name in 1910, was renamed for Marshal Gomes da Costa in 1935, then regained its name 41 days after the 1974 revolution. A road tunnel opened underneath in 1997 and was extended in 2009. A redesign begun in 2023 added cycle routes, crossings and trees.',
 facts: ['The square was laid out under Diogo de Sousa in the early 16th century.', 'Its first name, Campo de Santana, referred to Saint Anne’s chapel.', 'Its area exceeds Avenida dos Aliados in Porto and Terreiro do Paço in Lisbon.', 'Avenida da Liberdade regained its name 41 days after the 1974 revolution.', 'The 1.15 km tunnel beneath Avenida da Liberdade opened in 1997.'],
 tip: 'TUB bus 2 to Bom Jesus leaves from Avenida da Liberdade (25 de Abril). Start with coffee under the Arcada, then walk east through the gardens of Avenida Central.',
 gallery: ['The boulevard’s fountain reflecting houses and towers', 'The bandstand on Avenida Central', 'Historic façades along the boulevard', 'The wide pedestrian promenade'],
},
'populo': {
 name: 'Pópulo Church and Convent', year: '1596', short: 'An Augustinian church with Carlos Amarante’s twin towers and walls of blue-and-white tiles',
 long: 'Archbishop Agostinho de Jesus laid the first stone in 1596 and chose the church as his burial place. In the late 18th century Carlos Amarante gave it a new twin-towered front. Blue-and-white tiles line the nave and chapels, and the altars glow with gilded carving. The former convent now houses offices of Braga City Council.',
 history: 'The name comes from Rome. Archbishop Agostinho de Jesus was an Augustinian friar with a special devotion to the image of the Virgin in Santa Maria del Popolo. In 1595 he endowed the Hermit Friars of Saint Augustine with land and income, and the first stone of the church and college was laid in 1596. He died in 1609 and was buried here, as he wished; his successor Aleixo de Meneses also lies in the chancel.\n\nBuilding went on for almost two centuries: a new cloister from 1706, the convent front from 1755, an organ in 1775. The old church front was close to collapse, so in the late 18th century it was taken down and rebuilt to a design by Carlos Amarante. The result is a Neoclassical portal between two columns, a large balconied window and two towers crowned with Baroque cupolas.\n\nInside, a single nave rises under a coffered stone vault with four transverse arches. Blue-and-white narrative tiles, attributed to António de Oliveira Bernardes, cover the side chapels and the chancel. Gilded Baroque altarpieces line the walls, and Our Lady of the Pópulo stands at the centre of the high altar.\n\nPortugal abolished the religious orders in 1834, and from 1841 the 8th Infantry Regiment occupied the convent. On 26 May 1926 General Gomes da Costa marched out from here in the uprising that led to the dictatorship; his statue stands in the square outside. City Council departments moved in from 1990, and in 2020–2021 the church was restored at a cost of almost one million euros.',
 facts: ['The first stone was laid in 1596.', 'The church is named after Santa Maria del Popolo in Rome.', 'Its chapel tiles are attributed to António de Oliveira Bernardes.', 'The 26 May 1926 uprising of General Gomes da Costa set out from here.', 'Church and convent have been listed as a Property of Public Interest since 1977.'],
 tip: 'The church faces Praça Conde de Agrolongo, three minutes from Biscainhos Palace. Walk round to the south side for the Pópulo fountain of 1844. The tiled staircase and convent cloister can be seen on guided tours booked in advance.',
 gallery: ['The nave under its coffered vault, with blue-and-white tiled chapels', 'Looking down the nave to the high altar', 'A gilded carved altarpiece with an image of the Virgin', 'The organ in its gilded case above the choir', 'The convent cloister with its two-storey arcade'],
},
'ucp-braga': {
 name: 'Portuguese Catholic University – Braga Campus', short: 'Braga’s oldest university: the Jesuits’ philosophy faculty, the Catholic University’s first',
 long: 'The Jesuits opened a philosophy institute in Braga in 1934, and in 1947 it became a pontifical faculty. In 1967 it joined the new Portuguese Catholic University as its first faculty. The campus now teaches philosophy, social sciences and theology. Its main building stands on Praça da Faculdade, beside the Jesuit Church of the Sacred Heart.',
 history: 'The faculty sees itself as heir to the Jesuits, who taught in Braga from the 16th century. In 1934 the order opened the Blessed Miguel de Carvalho Institute of Philosophy on Rua de São Barnabé. Miguel de Carvalho was born in Braga in 1579 and died a martyr in Ōmura, Japan, in 1624. In 1942 the Ministry of Education recognised the institute’s course as higher education in philosophy.\n\nIn 1947 the institute became a pontifical faculty of philosophy, making it the oldest university institution in Braga. On 13 October 1967 the decree Lusitanorum nobilissima gens founded the Portuguese Catholic University, and the Braga faculty became its first. A theology faculty followed in 1968. It was also the first non-state institution in Portugal to award bachelor’s and doctoral degrees.\n\nA faculty of social sciences opened in Braga in 2001, and on 1 June 2015 it merged with philosophy to form the Faculty of Philosophy and Social Sciences. The campus has three groups of buildings: Campus Camões, owned by the university; Praça da Faculdade, owned by the Society of Jesus; and Rua de Santa Margarida, owned by the Archdiocese of Braga.\n\nThe Jesuits built the main building on Praça da Faculdade on the site of the São Vicente school, once donated to the parish by the Count of Ferreira. Beside it stands the small Church of the Sacred Heart, which belongs to the neighbouring Jesuit house and is open for worship. Inside stands a statue of Pedro Hispano, the 13th-century philosopher and physician who became Archbishop of Braga and later Pope John XXI.',
 facts: ['Founded in 1947, the Faculty of Philosophy is Braga’s oldest university institution.', 'In 1967 it became the Catholic University’s first faculty.', 'The 1934 institute was named after the martyr Miguel de Carvalho.', 'Philosophy and social sciences merged into one faculty in 2015.'],
 tip: 'The campus is a five-minute walk north of Avenida Central, beside the Campo Novo garden. It is a working university, so go inside only on business; the towered front and the Sacred Heart church are best seen from Praça da Faculdade.',
 gallery: ['The faculty’s main hall with rows of seats', 'Statue of Pedro Hispano, Archbishop of Braga and Pope John XXI', 'The inscription by the statue: Dante’s line on Pedro Hispano'],
},
'estadio-1-maio': {
 name: '1st of May Stadium', short: 'A 1950 granite stadium with a tower over the gate and bronze reliefs by Barata Feyo',
 long: 'The stadium opened on 28 May 1950 and was named after the 1926 coup. Stone stands ring the pitch and running track. A slender granite tower rises over the main entrance, flanked by bronze reliefs. Sporting Braga played here for half a century, and the stadium is now a listed monument.',
 history: 'In the early 20th century Sporting Braga played on rented grounds: Campo do Raio, Campo da Ponte, Campo dos Peões. The city needed a large stadium, and the state paid for it. The architect João Simões drew the design and the engineer Travassos Valdez directed the work. It was meant to rival the National Stadium at Jamor, and like it was built of stone. The first stone was laid in 1946, on the 21st anniversary of the 28 May 1926 coup.\n\nPresident Óscar Carmona and Prime Minister António de Oliveira Salazar opened the stadium on 28 May 1950 as the “28 May Stadium”. The day brought a sports parade and two matches: Braga drew with Porto, and Benfica beat Sporting. After the revolution of 25 April 1974 the stadium became the “1st of May”.\n\nThe plan is symmetrical, in granite and reinforced concrete. Open stands surround the pitch and athletics track, with floodlight masts at the corners. The north front, with rounded ends, is reached by a broad staircase. Above the entrance rises a slender prismatic tower bearing the arms of the Republic. Bronze sports reliefs by Barata Feyo flank it; one quotes a line from Camões.\n\nThe south stands decayed by 1990 and were repaired for the 1991 FIFA World Youth Championship. Braga played its last official match here on 21 December 2003, beating Vitória de Guimarães 2–1, before moving to the new Municipal Stadium. The 1st of May was listed as a Monument of Public Interest in 2012. Braga B and the women’s team played here until February 2025.',
 facts: ['The stadium opened on 28 May 1950 as the “28 May Stadium”.', 'Wikipedia gives 28,000 places; since 2012 the licence allows 5,000.', 'The bronze reliefs at the gate are attributed to the sculptor Barata Feyo.', 'Braga’s last official match here was on 21 December 2003.', 'It has been a Monument of Public Interest since 2012.'],
 tip: 'The stadium stands on the southern edge of Parque da Ponte. The tower and reliefs at the main gate can be seen from outside at any time. The park lake and Forum Braga are a couple of minutes away on foot.',
 gallery: ['Stone stands and pitch during an American football game', 'A bronze sports relief by the main gate', 'A portal with a coat of arms in a frame of laurel leaves', 'The tower over the gate and the glazed gallery to the boxes'],
},
'parque-ponte': {
 name: 'Parque da Ponte', short: 'Braga’s old city park by the River Este: a lake, a 1616 chapel and the São João festival',
 long: 'The park grew on the archbishops’ former woodland, which the city acquired in 1911. Its outer part holds the Chapel of São João da Ponte from 1616, a bandstand and Baroque stones from the demolished Remédios church. The inner part surrounds a large lake under sequoias and cedars. On the night of 23 June the whole city comes here to celebrate.',
 history: 'The name recalls the medieval bridge over the River Este on the old road to Guimarães; its stones were reused in the present crossing. In 1570, during a plague, Archbishop Bartolomeu dos Mártires built a house for the sick here. The Brotherhood of São João da Ponte was founded in 1615, and the chapel of Saint John the Baptist followed in 1616, as the date on its portal records. The land belonged to the archbishops, and the Sottomayor family helped pay for the building.\n\nThe park itself dates from 1911, when the city expropriated the Quinta da Mitra, the archbishops’ old woodland. Stones from demolished palaces and convents were set around the chapel: columns, capitals and figures from the façade of the Remédios church. A railing from the old public garden on Avenida Central divides off the inner park, with its large artificial lake, sequoias, limes, cedars, hollies, camellias and an artificial grotto.\n\nWork on a stadium at the southern edge began in 1946, and the Ponte sports complex grew around it, with a swimming pool and the Flávio Sá Leite pavilion. Further south lies the city’s only campsite. In 2010–2011 the outer park was renewed for about two million euros, to designs by the architect Sérgio Borges and the landscape architect Carlos Arantes; the scheme won the national IHRU prize for public spaces in 2011.\n\nThe park is the main stage of the São João festival. On the night of 23–24 June thousands of people carrying toy hammers stream to the chapel, and a street party fills the grounds. When the Este still ran clean, Braga’s women washed clothes on its banks and dried them on the slopes of Mount Picoto.',
 facts: ['The Chapel of São João da Ponte was built in 1616.', 'The city acquired the land, the archbishops’ old woodland, in 1911.', 'The renewed part covers 24,000 m²; the whole park is about 5.5 ha.', 'The 2010–2011 renewal won the national IHRU prize.', 'The main São João street party takes place here on 23–24 June.'],
 tip: 'The park begins at the end of Avenida da Liberdade, about 20 minutes on foot from Praça da República. Walk round the lake in the inner park, then head to the chapel and bandstand. The 1st of May Stadium and Forum Braga are next door.',
 gallery: ['The nave and high altar of the Chapel of São João da Ponte', 'A gilded pulpit against the chapel’s tiles', 'The iron bandstand on its stone base', 'Boats and kayaks on the lake in the inner park', 'Saint John the Baptist in a niche from the façade of the demolished Remédios church'],
},
'forum-braga': {
 name: 'Forum Braga', short: 'A 1981 exhibition centre, rebuilt in 2018 as a congress centre and concert hall',
 long: 'Braga’s exhibition park opened in 1981 beside the River Este. In 2017–2018 the practice Barbosa & Guimarães turned it into Forum Braga. It holds the largest auditorium in northern Portugal and a pavilion for 12,000 standing. The square outside hosts concerts for 20,000 people.',
 history: 'Braga’s exhibition park, the Parque de Exposições, was created in 1981. Its heart was the Grande Nave, a 100 × 50 m pavilion for fairs, exhibitions and sports events with room for 3,000 spectators. Beside it stood a congress centre with a 1,204-seat auditorium and 104 permanent stalls, which also hosted the city’s weekly Tuesday market.\n\nFrom 2014 the municipal company InvestBraga ran the park. In February 2017 the city commissioned a complete rebuild for eight million euros, and work began in June. The design came from Barbosa & Guimarães, led by the architect Pedro Guimarães. They kept the old buildings but cleared their plan and used thermally modified wood on the façades. Extra works pushed the cost to almost ten million euros.\n\nThe Forum opened in stages: the exhibition area hosted the Agro fair in May 2018, and the formal opening followed on 11 September 2018. Until April 2024 it carried a sponsor’s name, Altice Forum Braga. In 2021 it served as a major vaccination centre, where more than 270,000 people received COVID-19 jabs.\n\nInside, the 107.5 × 46.3 m pavilion holds up to 12,000 people standing and has retractable seating for 1,680. The 1,432-seat Grand Auditorium is the largest in the north of the country. There is also a 254-seat small auditorium, six congress rooms, a contemporary art gallery and parking for 620 cars. The name recalls the Roman forum of Bracara Augusta.',
 facts: ['The 107.5 × 46.3 m pavilion holds up to 12,000 standing.', 'The 1,432-seat Grand Auditorium is the largest in northern Portugal.', 'Barbosa & Guimarães designed the 2017–2018 rebuild.', 'From 2018 to 2024 it was called Altice Forum Braga.', 'Its open-air square hosts concerts for 20,000 people.'],
 tip: 'The Forum stands at the western edge of Parque da Ponte, five minutes from the 1st of May Stadium. Check forumbraga.com for concerts, fairs and congresses. The building also houses the Forum Arte Braga contemporary art gallery.',
 gallery: ['Rows of seats and the balcony of the Grand Auditorium', 'The pavilion under its steel trusses, with a first-floor gallery', 'One of the congress centre rooms with lines of light', 'FORUM letters on the square outside the entrance'],
},
'uminho-gualtar': {
 name: 'University of Minho – Gualtar Campus', year: '1986–1993', short: 'The University of Minho’s main campus, with teaching blocks, a library and green lawns in east Braga',
 long: 'The University of Minho was founded in 1973, and its first classes began in 1975. Work on the Gualtar campus started in 1986, and the first phase opened in 1993. Today it is the university’s largest campus, home to future lawyers, doctors, psychologists, economists and scientists. Lawns, trees and a statue of Prometheus fill the spaces between the buildings.',
 history: 'The University of Minho was created on 11 August 1973 by Decree-Law 402/73, together with the universities of Aveiro and Nova de Lisboa. These “new universities” changed higher education in Portugal. The first classes took place in the 1975/76 academic year. Trainee teachers, language students and engineers were then spread across several buildings in Braga, including a teaching complex on Rua D. Pedro V.\n\nIn 1976 the government decided to split the university between two cities, Braga and Guimarães. The first stone of the Gualtar campus was laid in 1986, and the first phase opened in 1993. The campus lies on the eastern edge of Braga, in the parish of Gualtar, on the right bank of the River Este. Local history holds that the medieval monastery of São Miguel de Gualtar once stood on this land.\n\nToday Gualtar is the university’s largest campus. It houses the schools of law, sciences, psychology, medicine, nursing, and economics and management; the institutes of social sciences, education and the humanities; and part of the school of engineering. There are three teaching complexes on the campus. Nearby stand the General Library, a canteen, a medical post and a sports complex. The rectorate remains in the city centre, on Largo do Paço.\n\nIn 2025 the university had 21,000 students across Braga and Guimarães. Since 2016 it has been a public foundation run under private law. In 2023 it was made an honorary member of the Order of Public Instruction.',
 facts: ['The university was founded on 11 August 1973; its first classes began in 1975/76.', 'The first stone of the Gualtar campus was laid in 1986; the first phase opened in 1993.', 'Gualtar is the university’s largest campus; its second city is Guimarães.', 'In 2025 the university had 21,000 students.', 'In 2023 it became an honorary member of the Order of Public Instruction.'],
 tip: 'The campus lies in the parish of Gualtar, in east Braga, and you can walk around it freely. Head from the Prometheus statue to the General Library and the teaching complexes. A building map is on campi.uminho.pt.',
 gallery: ['Building 4, with a glass façade and concrete towers', 'The façade of the university’s General Library', 'The glass entrance of the General Library, reflecting the campus courtyard', 'The Prometheus statue on the campus square'],
},
'dmaria-ii': {
 name: 'D. Maria II Secondary School', year: '1964', short: 'A 1964 girls’ lyceum; boys joined in 1973, and it became a secondary school in 1974',
 long: 'The D. Maria II Lyceum was created in Braga in spring 1964 as a girls’ lyceum with 24 classrooms. In its first year it took 1,014 pupils. Boys were admitted in 1973, and after the 1974 revolution it became a secondary school. In its first half-century, more than 80,000 people studied here.',
 history: 'Decree-Law 45636 of 31 March 1964 created the D. Maria II National Lyceum in Braga. The decree describes it as “a girls’ lyceum with 24 classrooms”. The school is named after Queen Maria II (1819–1853). Its address today is Rua 25 de Abril, 198, a few minutes’ walk from the historic centre.\n\nIn the first school year, 1,014 girls attended day classes. Single-sex teaching lasted until 1973, when the education reform of minister Veiga Simão opened the lyceum to boys. By 1974 there were already 1,911 pupils in mixed classes. After the revolution of 25 April 1974, the lyceum was renamed D. Maria II Secondary School, the name it still bears.\n\nThe busiest years came after the revolution. In 1975/76 the day classes had 2,703 pupils, and in 1981/82, counting evening classes, the total reached 2,885. More than 80,000 people studied here in the first 50 years. General courses that prepare pupils for university have always been the school’s core. Since 2005 it has also offered vocational secondary courses, and later it added evening classes for adults.\n\nThe school later became the head of the D. Maria II school group, which also includes the Lamaçães basic school. In 2014 the group had about 3,200 pupils. The state company Parque Escolar rebuilt the school in depth. The contractor put up new blocks for laboratories, workshops, a library, a sports hall and an auditorium, and renovated the older buildings. Work went ahead in phases so that the school never closed.',
 facts: ['The lyceum was created by Decree-Law 45636 of 31 March 1964.', 'The decree set it up as a girls’ lyceum with 24 classrooms.', 'Boys were admitted in 1973, after minister Veiga Simão’s reform.', 'It has carried its current name, D. Maria II Secondary School, since 1974.', 'More than 80,000 people studied here in its first 50 years.'],
 tip: 'This is a working school, and visitors cannot go inside. You can see the buildings from Rua 25 de Abril, about a 10-minute walk from Praça da República.',
 gallery: ['The school’s long four-storey block with rows of windows'],
},
'sao-frutuoso': {
 name: 'Chapel of São Frutuoso de Montélios', year: '7th–10th centuries', short: 'An early medieval cross-shaped mausoleum chapel beside a Franciscan convent',
 long: 'Tradition says that Saint Fructuosus, Bishop of Braga, built a chapel here in the 7th century as his tomb. Many historians now date the present building to the early 10th century. Inside, horseshoe arches rest on columns, and a dome crowns the centre of the cross. A Franciscan convent founded in 1523 stands beside it, and the chapel has been a National Monument since 1944.',
 history: 'Fructuosus was a Visigothic monk who founded many monasteries. He became Bishop of Dumio in 654 and Bishop of Braga in 656. Tradition holds that he founded the monastery of São Salvador on the hill of Montélios and ordered a chapel built as his tomb. His disciple Valerius wrote that the saint was buried here; Fructuosus died in 665. The name Montélios means “little hill”.\n\nThe building date is disputed. The chapel was rebuilt and redecorated in the 9th and 10th centuries. Portugal’s state culture service considers the present building more likely to date from the early 10th century, when the bishop’s cult revived during Alfonso III’s resettlement of the region. The plan is a Greek cross: rectangular on the outside, while inside three arms end in semicircular apses. Triple arcades with horseshoe arches frame them, and a dome rises over the crossing. Historians compare the plan with the Mausoleum of Galla Placidia in Ravenna.\n\nIn 1102 Diego Gelmírez, Archbishop of Compostela, carried the saint’s relics off to Santiago. In 1523 Archbishop Diogo de Sousa founded a Franciscan convent next door and refused to let the chapel be demolished. In the 18th century it was built into the new church of the São Francisco convent and heavily altered. The architect Ernesto Korrodi described the monument in 1897, and restoration to the original plan began in 1931. The chapel has been a National Monument since 1944.\n\nIn 1966 Compostela returned the relics to Braga, where they were kept in the cathedral. On 18 October 2025 they were moved back to the chapel, more than 900 years after they left. In 2024 a €2.5 million restoration of the convent was completed. The visitor route now covers two floors of the convent, the mausoleum, the church and the sacristy.',
 facts: ['The chapel’s plan is a Greek cross with a dome over the crossing.', 'Portugal’s state culture service dates the building more likely to the early 10th century.', 'In 1102 the saint’s relics were taken to Santiago de Compostela.', 'Saint Fructuosus’s relics returned to the chapel on 18 October 2025.', 'The chapel has been a Portuguese National Monument since 1944.'],
 tip: 'The chapel stands in the parish of Real, about 2 km north-west of the centre. You enter through the church of São Francisco; the chapel lies below its floor. Check opening times in advance: since the 2024 restoration the convent receives visitors on guided routes.',
 gallery: ['A view through the triple horseshoe arcades towards the exit', 'The dome over the crossing', 'A triple arcade on columns in front of one of the apses', 'A carved leaf frieze above the columns', 'The façade of the church of São Francisco, beside the chapel'],
},
'diogo-sousa': {
 name: 'D. Diogo de Sousa Archaeology Museum', year: '1918, 2007', short: 'The archaeology museum of Roman Bracara Augusta, from the Palaeolithic to the Middle Ages',
 long: 'The museum was founded in 1918 but got a building of its own only in 2007. The architects Carlos Guimarães and Luís Soares Carneiro placed it in the most important archaeological zone of Roman Bracara Augusta. The collection covers northern Portugal from the Palaeolithic to the Middle Ages. It holds one of Europe’s finest collections of Roman milestones.',
 history: 'The museum is named after Diogo de Sousa (1461–1532), Archbishop of Braga from 1505. He remodelled the city and was among the first to protect the heritage of Roman Bracara Augusta. The archbishop gathered Roman milestones and set them up on the Campo de Sant’Ana, today’s Avenida Central, creating the city’s first open-air “museum”. In 1769 Archbishop Gaspar de Bragança moved the stones to the Alameda das Carvalheiras.\n\nThe town council decided to create a museum as early as 1892, but the decision stayed on paper. The museum was officially founded by a decree of 28 March 1918 as a museum of archaeology and art. It occupied rooms in the Archbishop’s Palace but did not work regularly until 1980. That year it was revived as the Regional Archaeology Museum.\n\nWork on a new building began around 1990. It soon stalled: the new heads of the museum service called the project a “white elephant”. Work resumed in 2001, and the museum opened on 29 June 2007. The design is by Carlos Guimarães and Luís Soares Carneiro, architects of the Porto School. The building has three wings: a technical wing with a conservation laboratory, a café, and a public wing with galleries, an auditorium, a library and a shop. The gardens around it are open to all.\n\nThe collection holds more than 30,000 objects. The Roman milestones of the Capela inventory have been a National Monument since 1910. One Roman mosaic floor is shown where it was found. In 2018–2019 the German patrons Bühler-Brockhaus donated almost 300 ancient objects. Among them is a bust of Emperor Augustus made in his lifetime, the only one in Portugal.',
 facts: ['The museum was founded by a decree of 28 March 1918.', 'The new building opened on 29 June 2007.', 'The collection holds more than 30,000 objects, from the Palaeolithic to the Middle Ages.', 'A lifetime bust of Augustus from the Bühler-Brockhaus gift is the only one in Portugal.', 'The milestones of the Capela inventory have been a National Monument since 1910.'],
 tip: 'The museum is open Tuesday to Sunday, 10:00–17:30, and a full ticket costs €5. The gardens around the building are free. The Roman baths on the Alto da Cividade are close by.',
 gallery: ['A gallery of ancient vases and sculpture in glass cases', 'A statue of Emperor Augustus in the museum’s bright entrance hall', 'Iron Age gold torcs', 'The mosaic “The Wedding of Pelops and Hippodamia”', 'The museum’s white wings and paved courtyard'],
},
'coimbras': {
 name: 'Coimbras Chapel and House', year: '1525–1528', short: 'A 16th-century tower chapel with a carved stone altarpiece and tiles of the Creation',
 long: 'João de Coimbra, vicar-general of the Archdiocese of Braga, built this tower-like chapel in 1525–1528. Outside it has battlements, statues and a porch closed by a wrought-iron grille. Inside are a ribbed vault, a painted stone altarpiece and blue-and-white tiles. The neighbouring Coimbras House keeps its Manueline windows: it was taken down in the early 20th century and rebuilt beside the chapel in 1924.',
 history: 'The founder was Dr João de Coimbra, vicar-general of the archdiocese under Archbishop Diogo de Sousa. In 1505 he bought a house beside the church of São João do Souto, and in 1525 he began a private chapel next to it, dedicated to the Immaculate Conception. A Latin inscription on the wall records that he consecrated it to the Mother of God in 1528. In 1530 he founded an entailed estate: his lands and houses passed to the eldest sons of the family, and the chapel became the family burial place.\n\nThe chapel looks like a square, battlemented tower. Masons from Biscay built it, and the sculptor Hodart carved the statues outside. In front of the door stands a columned porch with a wrought-iron grille and the Coimbra arms; above the portal are the arms of Archbishop Diogo de Sousa.\n\nInside, gilded rosettes stud the ribbed vault, with the founder’s arms at the centre. The Renaissance stone altarpiece is attributed to the young João de Ruão. Its niches hold painted figures of the Virgin and Child, Saint Anne and Saint Joachim. Nearby is a sculpted Entombment of Christ, which gave the chapel its other name, Senhor Morto, “the Dead Lord”. Blue-and-white 18th-century tiles with scenes of the Creation cover the walls.\n\nThe chapel once adjoined the Romanesque church of São João do Souto. The church was later rebuilt, but the chapel stayed as it was, and in 1910 it became a National Monument. The Coimbras House stood about a hundred metres away. In the early 20th century the city cleared the square and the house was dismantled. In 1924 it was rebuilt beside the chapel, keeping its Manueline windows and doors. The founder’s descendants still gather here for Mass on 8 December, as João de Coimbra wished.',
 facts: ['A Latin inscription on the wall dates the chapel’s consecration to 1528.', 'The stone altarpiece is attributed to the young João de Ruão.', 'The chapel has been a National Monument since 16 June 1910.', 'The house was dismantled in the early 20th century and rebuilt in 1924.', 'The founder’s descendants gather here every year for Mass on 8 December.'],
 tip: 'The chapel stands on Largo de São João do Souto, three minutes’ walk from the cathedral. Entry is paid; check opening hours on the Visit Braga website. Look closely at the porch grille: cherubs hold up the Coimbra arms. São Marcos Church is a couple of minutes away on foot.',
 gallery: ['The stone altarpiece: the Virgin and Child between Saint Joachim and Saint Anne', 'The sculpted Entombment of Christ', 'The ribbed vault with gilded rosettes and the founder’s arms', 'Latin inscription: João de Coimbra consecrated the chapel to the Virgin in 1528', 'A Manueline window of the Coimbras House'],
},
'congregados': {
 name: 'Congregados Basilica', year: '18th century', short: 'A Baroque Oratorian church with a façade by André Soares; its second tower was finished only in 1964',
 long: 'The Oratorian friars laid the foundation of the church in 1703 and it was blessed in 1717. The façade, designed by André Soares, was built in 1761–1766, but the towers and statues were left unfinished. The west tower was completed only in 1964, paid for by the patron Nogueira da Silva. Since 1975 the church has held the title of minor basilica.',
 history: 'The Congregation of the Oratory of Saint Philip Neri came to Braga in 1686 at the invitation of Canon João de Meira Carrilho. The friars built a house and a college, and in 1703 they laid the foundation of the church, with the master builder Manuel Fernandes da Silva in charge of the work. With the permission of Archbishop Rodrigo de Moura Teles, the church was blessed on 27 October 1717.\n\nThe façade was designed by André Soares, the leading architect of Baroque Braga. The Galician stonemason Paulo Vidal built it in 1761–1766. Soares also drew the choir arch, the organ case and the altar of Our Lady of Sorrows. But the façade stayed unfinished: one tower stood without its dome, and the other rose only halfway. In 1769 the Marquis of Pombal ordered the church closed, and it did not reopen until 1777.\n\nIn 1834 the religious orders were abolished in Portugal, and the Oratorians’ house passed to the state. It held a public library, a lyceum and later a teacher training school; today the building houses the music department of the University of Minho. Since 1845 the church has served the Brotherhood of Our Lady of Sorrows. On 14 July 1944 a small plane clipped the east tower with its wing and crashed; the pilot was killed.\n\nThe façade was completed only in the 20th century. The patron António Augusto Nogueira da Silva paid for the west tower, designed by the architect Alberto da Silva Bessa; it was finished in 1964. That same year statues of Saint Philip Neri and Saint Martin of Dume, by Manuel da Silva Nogueira, were raised into the niches of the façade. On 15 March 1975 the Holy See gave the church the title of minor basilica.',
 facts: ['The church was founded in 1703 and blessed on 27 October 1717.', 'The façade, designed by André Soares, was built in 1761–1766.', 'The west tower was finished only in 1964.', 'In 1944 a plane clipped the east tower with its wing and crashed.', 'A minor basilica since 1975; a Property of Public Interest since 1993.'],
 tip: 'The basilica stands on Avenida Central, five minutes’ walk from Republic Square. Entry is free. A hundred metres across the road is the Nogueira da Silva Museum, founded by the man who paid for the west tower. The façade is beautifully lit in the evening.',
 gallery: ['The nave and main altar under the painted vault', 'The nave and altar seen from the choir', 'The organ in its gilded case in the choir', 'Saint Philip Neri in a niche of the façade, a statue from 1964', 'The basilica’s two towers in the evening light'],
},
'nogueira-silva': {
 name: 'Nogueira da Silva Museum', year: '1975', short: 'A patron’s house museum on Avenida Central: art from the 15th to the 20th century and a French-style garden',
 long: 'The businessman and collector António Nogueira da Silva bequeathed his house, garden and collection to the University of Minho in 1975. The house was built in the 1950s and 1960s to a design by Raul Rodrigues Lima. Inside are paintings, furniture, silver, Chinese porcelain, ivory and tapestries. Behind the house lies a garden with box-hedged parterres, statues and tiles.',
 history: 'António Augusto Nogueira da Silva (1901–1976) was born in Porto into a bourgeois Braga family; his relatives traded in wool and worked in banks. His fortune came from the Casa da Sorte, a lottery office he opened in Braga in 1933. Branches later spread across Portugal and the colonies. Even before the 1974 revolution he handed the firm over to its employees.\n\nNogueira da Silva gave generously to the city. He built a neighbourhood with a school, finished the Congregados basilica and helped found the Catholic University. In 1964 Braga made him an honorary citizen. The house on Avenida Central was designed by Raul Rodrigues Lima, the architect of cinemas and courthouses. It was built in two stages, in the 1950s and the 1960s, and planned for large receptions.\n\nIn September 1975 Nogueira da Silva bequeathed the house, garden and collection to the University of Minho, and in 1976 he died. He bought the pieces in the 1960s at auctions and from antique dealers. What to look for in the rooms: a painted 15th-century Italian cassone chest, Chinese export porcelain made for the European market, and a 17th-century Luso-Mughal ivory plaque. The main Portuguese painting is André Gonçalves’s Last Supper. The house also holds a contemporary art gallery, a photo library and an art history library.\n\nThe garden is laid out in the French manner: parterres framed by box hedges, magnolias and a Baroque fountain from an old manor house in Gualtar. Among the greenery stand ceramics by Jorge Barradas, two 18th-century Dutch tile panels and a 19th-century copy of Bernini’s Apollo and Daphne.',
 facts: ['The house, garden and collection were bequeathed to the University of Minho in September 1975.', 'The house was built in the 1950s and 1960s to a design by Raul Rodrigues Lima.', 'The rarest piece is a painted 15th-century Italian cassone chest.', 'Nogueira da Silva paid for the west tower of the Congregados basilica.', 'The Baroque fountain in the garden was brought from an old manor house in Gualtar.'],
 tip: 'The museum stands at Avenida Central 61, almost opposite the Congregados basilica. Check opening hours and prices on the museum’s website: it is usually closed on Sundays, Mondays and public holidays. After the rooms, be sure to step out into the garden.',
 gallery: ['The marble staircase with brass handrails', 'The hall with crystal chandeliers and a tapestry', 'The study and library of the house’s owner', 'Marble statues along a garden path', 'Apollo and Daphne in the garden: a 19th-century copy of Bernini’s sculpture'],
},
'sao-marcos': {
 name: 'São Marcos Church and Hospital', year: '18th century', short: 'A Baroque hospital façade with statues of the apostles; a hotel now fills the former wards',
 long: 'Archbishop Diogo de Sousa founded the São Marcos hospital in 1508. In the 18th century Carlos Amarante gave it a church with two towers and a long façade with statues of the apostles on the balustrade. The hospital treated the townspeople until 2011. Since 2018 a hotel has occupied its north wing, while the church still belongs to the Misericórdia brotherhood.',
 history: 'The place has a long history. Archbishop Rodrigo da Cunha recorded a tradition: the Templar master Gualdim Pais brought the relics of Saint John Mark back from the Holy Land. The relics were placed in a chapel, became famous for healing, and a shelter for the sick grew up beside it. In 1508 Archbishop Diogo de Sousa replaced three small shelters with a single hospital, São Marcos. In 1559 Archbishop Bartolomeu dos Mártires handed it to the brotherhood of the Santa Casa da Misericórdia, which ran it until 1975.\n\nIn the 18th century the north wing and the church were built to a design by Carlos Amarante. The side wings were designed and built by the stonemason José Fernandes da Graça, known as Landim. Work on the church stalled for long periods. From 1794 it continued with money from Archbishop Caetano Brandão, who in 1798 also opened a surgical school at the hospital.\n\nWhat to look for on the façade. The church’s two towers rise above a long, symmetrical block. Full-length figures line the balustrade: the apostles Peter, Andrew, John and James, and other saints. In the niche above the entrance stands Saint Mark in a bishop’s vestments. Inside are a gilded main altar, a painted dome and a stone reliquary with the Latin inscription “Sacred bones of the divine John Mark”.\n\nThe façades of the hospital and church have been a Property of Public Interest since 1956. The hospital worked until 2011, when Braga’s new hospital opened. The Misericórdia leased part of the buildings to the Vila Galé hotel chain. The 123-room hotel opened in 2018, and its wings are named after three archbishops. The church, the São Bento chapel and the Misericórdia’s pharmacy were not part of the hotel.',
 facts: ['Archbishop Diogo de Sousa founded the hospital in 1508.', 'The church and the north wing were built to a design by Carlos Amarante.', 'The hospital took in the sick for more than 500 years, until 2011.', 'A 123-room hotel opened in the former hospital in 2018.', 'The façades of the hospital and church have been a Property of Public Interest since 1956.'],
 tip: 'The church stands on Praça Carlos Amarante, next to the Raio Palace. Entry is free, but it opens only briefly: about an hour in the morning on weekdays and Sundays, and it is closed on Saturdays. Check the hours on the Visit Braga website. The façade with its statues is best seen from the small garden in front of it.',
 gallery: ['The church’s gilded main altar', 'The reliquary inscribed “Sacred bones of the divine John Mark”', 'Statues of the apostles on the hospital balustrade', 'Saint Mark in the niche above the church entrance', 'The courtyard of the former hospital, now a hotel'],
},
'leonardo-da-vinci': {
 name: 'Leonardo da Vinci School', year: '1988', short: 'A private school in central Braga: pre-school, primary and the first two lower-secondary years',
 long: 'Colégio Leonardo da Vinci opened in 1988. It is a private school with a pre-school, a primary school and the 5th and 6th years. The main building stands at Rua Conselheiro Bento Miguel 10. A second building with a large plot, at Rua de Santa Margarida 75, was opened for the 5th and 6th years. The Sá de Miranda secondary school is next door.',
 history: 'Colégio Leonardo da Vinci was founded in 1988 in the centre of Braga, beside the Sá de Miranda secondary school. It received its definitive licence to operate, no. 259, on 11 June 1990. The owner is the company Pro-Educação, Educação Sociedade Lda. The school takes children of any faith, outlook or social background, as far as it has room.\n\nThe school grew across two addresses. The main building stands at Rua Conselheiro Bento Miguel 10. The second building, the “polo”, stands at Rua de Santa Margarida 75. In the school’s photo it is a three-storey house with five windows across its front; behind it lies a plot with a garden.\n\nToday the school has a pre-school, a primary school (years 1 to 4) and the 5th and 6th years. For pre-school it signs development contracts with the Ministry of Education; for the primary years and the 5th and 6th years it signs simple contracts. On its website the school calls the 5th and 6th years its newest stage and describes new premises: about 5,000 m² of fenced land with green spaces, and a hall for 250 people.\n\nThe school sees its task as more than teaching facts. It wants to raise independent children who can learn, communicate and choose with conscience. Opening hours are 07:45 to 19:00.',
 facts: ['The school opened in 1988; its definitive licence, no. 259, dates from 11 June 1990.', 'The main building is at Rua Conselheiro Bento Miguel 10; the second is at Rua de Santa Margarida 75.', 'Stages: pre-school, primary (years 1 to 4) and the 5th and 6th years.', 'The second building has about 5,000 m² of fenced land and a 250-seat hall.', 'It is open 07:45 to 19:00, beside the Sá de Miranda secondary school.'],
 tip: 'This is a working private school, so please do not enter uninvited. You can see the main building from Rua Conselheiro Bento Miguel, a few minutes’ walk from Praça da República. The Sá de Miranda secondary school and Colégio Dom Diogo de Sousa are close by.',
 gallery: [],
},
};
